pages={
'/':('Giant Hornet Filming in Japan | Hornet Hunter — Michael J. Trout','Film giant hornets in Japan with UnDaoDu, the monk Michael J. Trout. Over a decade of documentary experience, location knowledge in Ehime and hornet consultancy.',f'''<section class="hero"><div class="hero-copy"><p class="eyebrow">UNDAODU · MICHAEL J. TROUT · JAPAN</p><h1>Filming giant hornets?<br>Start with the monk.</h1><p class="intro">Over a decade of giant-hornet documentary work.<br>From Animal Planet to <em>Secrets of the Bees</em>.</p><p>UnDaoDu — the monk, filmmaker and giant-hornet specialist behind Hornet Hunter — brings together location knowledge, shoot coordination and close experience of hornets and Japanese honeybees. Based in Fukui, with long-standing filming relationships in beautiful rural Ehime, on Shikoku.</p>{cta}<p class="small"><a href="/work/">Explore the documentary work</a> · For producers, directors and natural-history crews.</p></div><figure><img src="/assets/michael-trout.webp" width="1200" height="900" alt="Michael J. Trout holding a large hornet comb during field work in Japan"><figcaption>Michael J. Trout · Hornet Hunter field archive</figcaption></figure></section><section class="credits-band" aria-label="Selected documentary work"><p class="eyebrow">SELECTED DOCUMENTARY WORK</p><div><a href="/work/killer-hornets-from-hell/"><strong>ANIMAL PLANET</strong><span>Killer Hornets from Hell · 2015</span></a><a href="/work/#smithsonian"><strong>SMITHSONIAN CHANNEL</strong><span>Killer Hornet Swarm · 2016</span></a><a href="/work/secrets-of-the-bees/"><strong>NATIONAL GEOGRAPHIC</strong><span>Secrets of the Bees · 2026</span></a></div></section><section class="feature"><div><p class="eyebrow">LATEST DOCUMENTARY · 2026</p><h2>Secrets of the Bees</h2><p>A hornet approaches. The honeybees respond. Filming that encounter takes knowledge of the animals, the location and the moment. The monk brought his hornet consultancy and bee-balling filming experience to Silverback Films’ National Geographic series.</p><p>The series premiered on National Geographic on 31 March 2026 and arrived on Disney+ on 1 April.</p><a class="text-link" href="/work/secrets-of-the-bees/">Explore the latest work</a></div><div>{trailer}</div></section><section class="section"><p class="eyebrow">ON LOCATION IN JAPAN</p><h2>The right person.<br>The right place. The shot.</h2><div class="columns"><div><span class="number">01</span><h3>Hornet field consultancy</h3><p>Work with a specialist whose documentary experience reaches back to the 2014 shoot for Animal Planet. Turn your hornet story into a focused field brief.</p></div><div><span class="number">02</span><h3>Locations & shoot coordination</h3><p>Find a route into rural Japan: reconnaissance, specialist introductions and practical filming logistics. Build on the monk’s long-standing work in Ehime.</p></div><div><span class="number">03</span><h3>Behaviour & sequences</h3><p>Bring close experience of giant hornets, Japanese honeybees and bee-balling filming to your sequence. Plan camera requirements around the behaviour you need to reveal.</p></div></div><a class="text-link" href="/filming/">Plan a giant hornet shoot</a></section><section class="journal-strip"><p class="eyebrow">FIELD NOTES</p><h2>What happens before<br>the camera rolls.</h2><p>Notes on reconnaissance, filming briefs and the craft behind natural-history sequences.</p><a class="text-link" href="/journal/">Read the field notes</a><p class="small"><a href="https://www.facebook.com/hornethunters/" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Hunting Murder Hornets with Mike Trout on Facebook</a></p></section>'''),
'/filming/':('Giant Hornet Filming & Field Consultancy in Japan | Hornet Hunter','Planning a giant hornet documentary shoot in Japan? Contact Michael J. Trout for hornet field consultancy, reconnaissance and production planning.',f'''<section class="page-head"><p class="eyebrow">FOR NATURAL-HISTORY PRODUCTIONS</p><h1>Film giant hornets<br>in Japan.</h1><p class="intro">Bring the brief. The monk brings the field experience.</p></section><section class="prose"><p>Hornet Hunter is the giant-hornet filmmaking practice of UnDaoDu — the monk, Michael J. Trout. His work spans on-screen participation, hornet consultancy, location planning and Japan production support. It began with beekeeping and the need to understand the hornets threatening his colonies; it grew into more than a decade of documentary work.</p><p>Looking for a “murder hornet” filmmaker? That widely used term often refers to the Asian giant hornet, <em>Vespa mandarinia</em>. Tell us the species and behaviour your story needs.</p><h2>Ehime: a place, and a working relationship</h2><p>Beautiful rural Ehime, on Shikoku, is central to the monk’s hornet-filming experience. His repeated work with beekeeper and hornet specialist Susumu Tokunaga brings local knowledge to location discussions and shoot planning. Based in Fukui today, the monk helps productions scope their work in Japan.</p><h2>From location scouting to the shoot</h2><ul><li>Discuss the species, behaviour, sequences and editorial objectives.</li><li>Scope reconnaissance and suitable locations with local specialists.</li><li>Plan field support, specialist introductions and production logistics.</li><li>Discuss giant hornet–honeybee interactions, including bee-balling sequences.</li></ul><h2>Send a useful first brief</h2><p>Include your production company, programme or working title, filming window, crew size, intended sequences, camera requirements and any deadlines. Rates, travel, permissions and practical arrangements are agreed after the brief is reviewed.</p><p>Wildlife activity, weather, access and permissions affect what can be filmed. A plan should include time for observation and alternatives.</p>{cta}<p><a href="/contact/">the production enquiry form</a></p></section>'''),
'/work/':('Documentary Work | Hornet Hunter — Michael J. Trout','Selected giant hornet documentary work, including Secrets of the Bees and Killer Hornets from Hell. Michael J. Trout’s filming and field consultancy in Japan.',f'''<section class="page-head"><p class="eyebrow">SELECTED DOCUMENTARY WORK</p><h1>From beekeeper<br>to documentary specialist.</h1><p class="intro">The monk’s giant-hornet work spans Animal Planet, Smithsonian Channel and National Geographic productions.</p></section><section class="feature"><div><p class="eyebrow">2026 · NATIONAL GEOGRAPHIC / SILVERBACK FILMS</p><h2>Secrets of the Bees</h2><p>Hornet consultancy and experience of filming Japanese honeybee defence. The latest chapter in the monk’s long-running documentary work in Japan.</p><a class="text-link" href="/work/secrets-of-the-bees/">View the feature</a></div><div>{trailer}</div></section><section class="prose"><p class="eyebrow">2015 · ANIMAL PLANET / HOFF PRODUCTIONS</p><h2>Killer Hornets from Hell</h2><p>Before the documentaries, there were the bees. The monk’s experience keeping Japanese honeybees and trying to protect them from hornets helped shape his story in this Animal Planet special, filmed in 2014 and released in 2015.</p><a class="text-link" href="/work/killer-hornets-from-hell/">Read the origin story</a><div id="smithsonian" class="work-entry"><p class="eyebrow">2016 · SMITHSONIAN CHANNEL</p><h2>Killer Hornet Swarm</h2><p>Another chapter in the monk’s giant-hornet documentary work in Japan, following the Animal Planet special.</p><p><a class="text-link" href="https://www.si.edu/object/two-giant-killer-hornet-colonies-fight-death%3Ayt_sJ5ymto8lWg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Explore the Smithsonian programme archive</a></p></div><h2>Put that experience to work.</h2><p>Planning a giant-hornet film? Send the monk the behaviour, dates and crew requirements. Start the conversation with someone who has already worked in this world.</p>{cta}</section>'''),
'/work/killer-hornets-from-hell/':('Killer Hornets from Hell — Animal Planet & Hoff Productions | Hornet Hunter','The beekeeping story behind Michael J. Trout’s participation in Killer Hornets from Hell, made by Hoff Productions for Animal Planet and released in 2015.',f'''<section class="page-head"><p class="eyebrow">ANIMAL PLANET · HOFF PRODUCTIONS · 2015</p><h1>Before the cameras,<br>there were the bees.</h1><p class="intro">Killer Hornets from Hell.<br>The first chapter of the monk’s documentary story.</p></section><section class="prose"><p>For UnDaoDu — the monk, Michael J. Trout — giant hornets were part of a beekeeper’s reality. Keeping Japanese honeybees brought him into contact with the hornets that could threaten his colonies. The question was personal: how could he better understand the hornets and protect the bees?</p><h2>A beekeeper’s experience becomes a story</h2><p>That experience helped shape his participation in <em>Killer Hornets from Hell</em>, produced by Hoff Productions for Animal Planet. Filmed in 2014 and released on 20 May 2015, the special introduced international audiences to giant hornets and the people working around them in Japan.</p><p>Contemporary newspaper coverage describes the monk’s Japanese beekeeping, his experience of hornet attacks on colonies and his work designing a hive to better protect the bees. His path into documentary filmmaking grew from that hands-on concern.</p><h2>More than a decade later</h2><p>The work continued through further giant-hornet productions in Japan and, most recently, hornet consultancy for <a href="/work/secrets-of-the-bees/">Secrets of the Bees</a>. Along the way, the monk developed long-standing filming relationships in rural Ehime and repeated experience working with Susumu Tokunaga.</p><h2>Planning your own film?</h2><p>Bring the monk your brief — the behaviour, the setting and the sequence you want to capture. His experience connects the animals, the location and the practical needs of a documentary crew.</p>{cta}<div class="source-note"><h3>Programme & contemporary coverage</h3><p><a href="https://www.hoff.tv/portfolio/killer-hornets-from-hell/" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Hoff Productions: programme page</a><br><a href="https://tv.apple.com/us/episode/killer-hornets-from-hell/umc.cmc.42ha02q2b1lzkkoin4uvk77ax" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Apple TV: release listing</a><br><a href="https://ufdcimages.uflib.ufl.edu/UF/00/02/83/15/03846/05-19-2015.pdf" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Citrus County Chronicle, 19 May 2015 (PDF)</a></p></div></section>'''),
'/work/secrets-of-the-bees/':('Secrets of the Bees | Giant Hornet Consultancy — Hornet Hunter','Michael J. Trout’s hornet consultancy for Silverback Films’ Secrets of the Bees, the 2026 National Geographic series. Watch the official trailer.',f'''<section class="page-head"><p class="eyebrow">NATIONAL GEOGRAPHIC · SILVERBACK FILMS · 2026</p><h1>Secrets of the Bees</h1><p class="intro">A remarkable defence.<br>A closer look at the world of bees.</p></section><section class="wide-video">{trailer}</section><section class="prose"><h2>Hornets, honeybees and field knowledge</h2><p>Michael J. Trout contributed as a hornet consultant to Silverback Films’ <em>Secrets of the Bees</em>. His repeated work with Susumu Tokunaga and experience filming Japanese honeybee bee-balling behaviour helped bring field knowledge to the production.</p><p>The series premiered on National Geographic on 31 March 2026, followed by Disney+ on 1 April. Availability varies by region.</p><h2>From the field to the screen</h2><p>The official trailer offers a first look at the series. A behind-the-scenes journal will grow with selected field photographs, production memories and material cleared for publication.</p><p>For a new production involving giant hornets or Japanese honeybees, send Michael your brief.</p>{cta}</section>'''),
'/about/':('Giant Hornet Filmmaker in Japan | UnDaoDu, Michael J. Trout','Meet UnDaoDu, Michael J. Trout: over a decade of giant hornet documentary work, field training with Susumu Tokunaga and filming support in Ehime and Fukui.',f'''<section class="page-head"><p class="eyebrow">UNDAODU · THE MONK · MICHAEL J. TROUT</p><h1>Trained in Japan.<br>Experienced on location.</h1><p class="intro">Over a decade filming giant hornets.<br>A beekeeper’s beginnings. An ongoing field education.</p></section><section class="about-grid"><img src="/assets/michael-trout.webp" width="1200" height="900" alt="Michael J. Trout holding a giant hornet comb during field work in Japan"><div><h2>From protecting bees to supporting film crews</h2><p>UnDaoDu — the monk, Michael J. Trout — first approached giant hornets through keeping Japanese honeybees. Protecting his colonies led him into a closer study of the animals and became part of his on-screen story in <a href="/work/killer-hornets-from-hell/">Killer Hornets from Hell</a>.</p><p>Since the 2014 Animal Planet shoot, his documentary experience has grown across hornet consultancy, location planning, filming support and production coordination. His work includes productions for Animal Planet, Smithsonian Channel and National Geographic, most recently <a href="/work/secrets-of-the-bees/">Secrets of the Bees</a>.</p><p>Based in Fukui, he brings long-standing field relationships in beautiful rural Ehime, on Shikoku, to crews looking to film giant hornets in Japan.</p></div></section><section class="section" id="teachers"><p class="eyebrow">TEACHERS & FIELD KNOWLEDGE</p><h2>Learning from<br>Susumu Tokunaga.</h2><div class="columns"><div><h3>Susumu Tokunaga · 徳永進</h3><p>Tokunaga is a beekeeper and hornet specialist based in Uchiko, Ehime. Michael’s field education includes learning from him and working together on hornet filming projects.</p></div><div><h3>Japanese television experience</h3><p>Tokunaga’s apiary archive records years of hornet and honeybee work for Japanese television, including <em>The! Tetsuwan! DASH!!</em>, NHK and TV Asahi.</p><a class="text-link" href="https://www.ikazaki.ne.jp/~toku/" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Explore Tokunaga’s own archive</a></div><div><h3>Experience passed on in the field</h3><p>The monk’s approach brings together beekeeping, learning alongside Japanese specialists and the practical demands of natural-history filming. He remains a student of the animals as well as a guide for visiting crews.</p><a class="text-link" href="/journal/from-beekeeper-to-hornet-filmmaker/">Read the field background</a></div></div></section><section class="section"><p class="eyebrow">CREW CARE</p><h2>A reported record<br>of no filming-team stings.</h2><p class="intro">Michael reports that no member of his filming team has been stung during the hornet shoots he has led.</p><p>Discuss the location, behaviour, crew requirements and safety planning with him before your shoot. Each production begins with its own field assessment.</p>{cta}</section>'''),
'/journal/':('Giant Hornet Filming Journal | Hornet Hunter Japan','Field notes on planning giant hornet filming in Japan, reconnaissance and natural-history production. The Hornet Hunter journal.', '''<section class="page-head"><p class="eyebrow">FIELD NOTES</p><h1>Before the camera.<br>Beyond the sequence.</h1><p class="intro">People, places and the work behind giant-hornet filming in Japan.</p></section><section class="about-grid"><img src="/assets/michael-trout.webp" width="1200" height="900" alt="Michael J. Trout with a hornet comb from the Hornet Hunter field archive"><div><p class="eyebrow">FROM THE FIELD ARCHIVE</p><h2>From beekeeper<br>to hornet filmmaker.</h2><p>The monk’s beginnings, his field education with Susumu Tokunaga and over a decade supporting documentary shoots.</p><a class="text-link" href="/journal/from-beekeeper-to-hornet-filmmaker/">Explore the field background</a></div></section><section class="journal-list"><a href="/journal/planning-a-hornet-shoot/"><span class="eyebrow">PRODUCTION NOTES · 6 MIN READ</span><h2>Planning a giant hornet shoot in Japan</h2><p>What to put in the brief, and why reconnaissance belongs in the schedule.</p><span>Read the field note</span></a><a href="/journal/bee-balling-filming/"><span class="eyebrow">BEHAVIOUR & FILM · 4 MIN READ</span><h2>Filming a honeybee defence sequence</h2><p>Start with a behavioural question, then plan what the camera needs to reveal.</p><span>Read the field note</span></a></section>'''),
'/journal/planning-a-hornet-shoot/':('Planning a Giant Hornet Shoot in Japan | Hornet Hunter Journal','A practical briefing guide for producers planning giant hornet filming in Japan: behaviour, reconnaissance, camera needs and production logistics.',f'''<article class="prose article"><p class="eyebrow">PRODUCTION NOTES · HORNET HUNTER</p><h1>Planning a giant hornet shoot in Japan</h1><p class="intro">A useful brief starts with what the audience should see.</p><p>“We want to film giant hornets” is a starting point. A stronger brief describes the behaviour, the story and the images needed to connect them. A nest sequence, a foraging sequence and an interaction with honeybees ask different questions of a field team.</p><h2>Define the sequence</h2><p>Identify the species or subject, the behaviour you hope to record and how the scene fits into the programme. Separate essential images from optional ones. Share references and camera requirements early so the field plan can respond to them.</p><h2>Give reconnaissance its own time</h2><p>Reconnaissance helps assess activity, access, the surroundings and practical camera positions. It is part of the production work. Conditions can change between a location visit and a filming day, so allow room to reassess.</p><h2>Plan for Japan, and for the field</h2><p>Discuss local permissions, specialist support, travel, interpretation and the proposed crew footprint. Include contingency time for weather and wildlife activity. Confirm responsibility for field risk assessment and emergency arrangements before the shoot.</p><h2>Start the conversation</h2><p>Send your filming window, crew size, intended behaviour, equipment needs and deadline. Those details make it possible to discuss a realistic scope and the next steps.</p>{cta}</article>'''),
'/journal/bee-balling-filming/':('Filming Japanese Honeybee Bee-Balling Behaviour | Hornet Hunter','Planning a documentary sequence about Japanese honeybee defence against hornets: observation, context and camera requirements.',f'''<article class="prose article"><p class="eyebrow">BEHAVIOUR & FILM · HORNET HUNTER</p><h1>Filming a honeybee defence sequence</h1><p class="intro">The action matters. So does the context around it.</p><p>A honeybee colony’s response to a hornet can become a compelling natural-history sequence. For a production team, the challenge is to show enough context for viewers to understand what is happening, alongside the close detail that makes the behaviour visible.</p><h2>Work backwards from the question</h2><p>What should the audience understand: the approaching hornet, the colony’s response, or the defensive ball itself? Define that question before deciding on lenses, angles and the coverage needed to tell the story.</p><h2>Observation before equipment</h2><p>Field observation helps the crew understand the location and activity. It also helps distinguish a hoped-for sequence from something the team can reasonably plan around. The filming schedule needs time for that work.</p><h2>Close detail, wider context</h2><p>Discuss both close imagery and the surrounding scene. If thermal imaging is part of the brief, plan it as a specialist requirement and agree how the images will be interpreted. A visual effect alone is not an explanation of the behaviour.</p><h2>A recent production</h2><p>Michael’s hornet consultancy for <a href="/work/secrets-of-the-bees/">Secrets of the Bees</a> builds on his experience supporting bee-balling filming in Japan. The official trailer is available on the programme feature page.</p>{cta}</article>'''),
'/journal/from-beekeeper-to-hornet-filmmaker/':('From Beekeeper to Giant Hornet Filmmaker | Hornet Hunter Field Notes','Michael J. Trout’s beekeeping beginnings, learning with Susumu Tokunaga in Ehime and over a decade of giant hornet documentary work.',f'''<section class="page-head"><p class="eyebrow">FIELD BACKGROUND · PEOPLE & PLACE</p><h1>From beekeeper<br>to hornet filmmaker.</h1><p class="intro">UnDaoDu, Michael J. Trout — learning in the field, filming in Japan.</p></section><article class="prose"><figure><img src="/assets/michael-trout.webp" width="1200" height="900" alt="Michael J. Trout holding a hornet comb in Japan"><figcaption>Michael J. Trout · Hornet Hunter field archive. The photograph’s exact shoot date is not recorded here.</figcaption></figure><h2>It began with Japanese honeybees</h2><p>For the monk, giant hornets were first a question of beekeeping: understanding the animals that threatened his honeybee colonies. That experience helped shape his involvement in <a href="/work/killer-hornets-from-hell/">Animal Planet’s Killer Hornets from Hell</a>, filmed in 2014 and released in 2015.</p><h2>Learning with Susumu Tokunaga</h2><p>His field education includes learning from Susumu Tokunaga (徳永進), the beekeeper and hornet specialist based in Uchiko, Ehime, and working together on filming projects. Tokunaga’s <a href="https://www.ikazaki.ne.jp/~toku/" target="_blank" rel="noopener">own apiary archive</a> records his hornet and honeybee work for Japanese television, including <em>The! Tetsuwan! DASH!!</em>.</p><h2>Over a decade behind the sequences</h2><p>Documentary work has taken Michael from his beekeeping beginnings into hornet consultancy, location planning, filming support and production coordination. His selected work includes Animal Planet, Smithsonian Channel and National Geographic’s <a href="/work/secrets-of-the-bees/">Secrets of the Bees</a>.</p><p>He is based in Fukui and maintains long-standing field relationships in rural Ehime. For a visiting crew, those relationships and accumulated field experience are part of planning a practical shoot in Japan.</p><h2>The people behind the camera matter too</h2><p>Michael reports that no member of his filming team has been stung during the hornet shoots he has led. Crew requirements and safety planning belong in the production conversation from the beginning.</p><p><a href="/about/#teachers">Meet the monk and his field teacher</a></p>{cta}</article>'''),

}

